@4.5 BMX.INC.BL -- a BMX bitmap into VERA
.
=4.5 BMX.INC.BL -- A BMX BITMAP INTO VERA
.
=DESCRIPTION
.
.  BMX.OPEN
.      IN: BMX.FILE$
.      OUT: BMX.ERROR$, and the header -- BMX.WIDTH .HEIGHT .DEPTH .VERSION
.        .PALUSED .PALFIRST .DATAOFF .PACKED
.
.  BMX.PAINT
.      IN: an open file
.      OUT: palette and pixels in VRAM, file closed
.
.  BMX.SHOW
.      IN: BMX.FILE$
.      OUT: both of the above
.
.  BMX.CLOSE
.      IN: --
.      OUT: abandons a file opened by BMX.OPEN
.
.  BMX.RESTORE
.      IN: --
.      OUT: puts the machine's own palette back
.
.BMX.ERROR$ empty means it worked.
.
:BMX.FILE$ = "TITLE.BMX"
:SCREEN 128
:GOSUB BMX.SHOW
:IF BMX.ERROR$ <> "" THEN PRINT BMX.ERROR$
.
.The careful form, when you want the size before committing to a screen mode,
.or want to report a bad file without the display already torn down:
.
:BMX.FILE$ = F$
:GOSUB BMX.OPEN
:IF BMX.ERROR$ <> "" THEN GOTO COMPLAIN
:' ... BMX.WIDTH, BMX.HEIGHT, BMX.PALUSED readable here ...
:SCREEN 128
:GOSUB BMX.PAINT
.
.  | Set the screen mode BEFORE BMX.PAINT, not after. SCREEN reloads the
.  | default palette, so a mode change after painting throws the image's
.  | colours away -- right pixels, wrong colours, which reads as a corrupt
.  | file rather than a mistake.
.
.The palette is borrowed, not taken. BMX.PAINT writes the picture's colours
.over the machine's, and nothing on the X16 puts them back -- not SCREEN, not
.the KERNAL's screen_mode. So BMX.PAINT stashes all 256 entries in spare VRAM
.first, and BMX.RESTORE writes them back:
.
:BMX.FILE$ = "TITLE.BMX"
:SCREEN 128
:GOSUB BMX.SHOW
:' ... the title screen ...
:GOSUB BMX.RESTORE
.
.A program that owns the machine can simply never call it. One that hands the
.machine back -- to BASIC, or to the next program in a chain -- calls it and
.is done. It is safe at any time: nothing kept means nothing to do, so a
.program whose picture failed to load can still call it on the way out. All
.256 entries are kept rather than the range the file claims, and kept once
.per run, which is what makes a slideshow restore what the machine had rather
.than the previous picture's leftovers.
.
.BMX.STASH is where they go -- VRAM $13000 by default, free above the 320x240
.framebuffer in the mode this module paints into. A caller with tiles,
.sprites or a second bitmap up there must move it, or switch it off with
.BMX.STASH = -1. It is checked rather than trusted: a stash inside the bitmap
.or running into the PSG registers is reported, and nothing is painted.
.
.  | -1 and not 0 to switch it off. Zero is what the variable holds before
.  | anyone has touched it, and BMX.INIT runs at the first BMX.OPEN -- long
.  | after a caller would have set it -- so 0 has to keep meaning "never
.  | set".
.
.8 bits per pixel, uncompressed, which is what SCREEN 128 shows without a
.decompressor. Anything else is reported, not attempted. The image is
.centred; larger than 320x240 is clipped.
.
.Examples: BMXVIEW.EXP.BL, and BMXPAL.EXP.BL for the palette
.
=EXAMPLE PROGRAM
.
.  BMXVIEW.EXP.BL -- runnable, in the GPC-BASIC folder
.  BMXPAL.EXP.BL -- runnable, in the GPC-BASIC folder
.
=SEE ALSO
.
